WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:02.901
-Keira, welcome back to the --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:04.701
Yeah, they love you.
-Wow!

00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:06.834
Hey!
-Yes!

00:00:06.834 --> 00:00:08.734
-Look -- people.
-People.

00:00:08.734 --> 00:00:09.901
-People.
Isn't that nice?

00:00:09.901 --> 00:00:11.267
Isn't it the greatest thing
in the world.

00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:12.467
The best audience ever.
-Yeah, people are great.

00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:13.501
Yep.
-Yeah.

00:00:13.501 --> 00:00:14.834
You look fantastic.

00:00:14.834 --> 00:00:16.534
How are you doing?
How is the family?

00:00:16.534 --> 00:00:18.200
-The family are okay.
-Yeah, they're good, yeah.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.367
-You know, they're --
Yeah, they're okay.

00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:20.601
-How old are they now --
the kids?

00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:22.234
-3 and 7.
-Love it.

00:00:22.234 --> 00:00:23.334
-Yeah, they were trying to --

00:00:23.334 --> 00:00:24.667
They're not happy about me
being here.

00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:25.767
-They're not?
-No.

00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:27.267
I said,
"I've got to go to New York."

00:00:27.267 --> 00:00:30.834
They said, "Why?" I said, "Well,
I'm gonna be on television."

00:00:30.834 --> 00:00:32.834
And my 3-year-old
doesn't get that.

00:00:32.834 --> 00:00:35.367
My 3-year-old is like,
"In da television?"

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:36.934
[ Laughter ]
And I'm like, "Yes."

00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:39.968
And she's like, "But how do you
get in da television?"

00:00:39.968 --> 00:00:42.868
"Well, I get -- in New York,
I get in the television."

00:00:42.868 --> 00:00:44.133
But, you know -- so, but --
-Yeah, it's a camera.

00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:45.534
Yeah, you can't explain.
-Yeah, the whole camera thing --

00:00:45.534 --> 00:00:46.868
They couldn't explain.
-No.

00:00:46.868 --> 00:00:49.968
What are they into right now?
-Oh, rainbow unicorns.

00:00:49.968 --> 00:00:51.067
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:00:51.067 --> 00:00:52.901
Rainbow unicorns.
-Everything.

00:00:52.901 --> 00:00:54.400
Everything rainbow unicorns.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.801
-I had a feeling that you would
say that they're into that.

00:00:56.801 --> 00:00:59.334
-Yeah.
-Would you like to --

00:00:59.334 --> 00:01:00.601
[ Audience "Awws" ]
-Oh, look.

00:01:00.601 --> 00:01:02.567
-Just in case --
-Shall I, for her?

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:03.901
Because she did actually
say to me --

00:01:03.901 --> 00:01:05.934
she said, "If you are going
in the television,

00:01:05.934 --> 00:01:08.267
you have to be dressed
like rainbow unicorn."

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:12.033
And I was like, "Well, I'm not
gonna do that, obviously."

00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:15.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.968
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:16.968 --> 00:01:18.234
Yes!
-So, yes.

00:01:18.234 --> 00:01:19.901
-Mom of the Year!
-Mum of the Year.

00:01:19.901 --> 00:01:22.734
-Your mom loves you so much!
-Right?

00:01:22.734 --> 00:01:24.834
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:24.834 --> 00:01:27.300
Delilah Belle Righton,
I love you.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.901
Can I take it off now?
-Yes. Yeah, of course.

00:01:28.901 --> 00:01:30.100
Yes, of course.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.267
It was just for your daughter.

00:01:31.267 --> 00:01:32.601
-Thank you, thank you.
I appreciate that.

00:01:32.601 --> 00:01:35.467
-Do they understand
why you're in "da television"?

00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:37.267
-I mean, yeah,
I've tried to explain films.

00:01:37.267 --> 00:01:38.501
Actually, they've seen
one of the --

00:01:38.501 --> 00:01:40.801
I did a version of
"The Nutcracker" a few years ago

00:01:40.801 --> 00:01:44.267
where I play, like,
a psychotic pink cake.

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:46.367
And so --
And so, they watched that.

00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:47.801
-Wow.
-Yeah, but she didn't

00:01:47.801 --> 00:01:49.067
like that at all.
She was like,

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:51.367
"A, you should be dressed
in rainbows."

00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:54.100
And she was,
"Why are you so angry?"

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.400
[ Laughter ]
And I was like, "I don't know."

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.767
And then the other one,
I tried to show her

00:01:56.767 --> 00:01:58.334
"Pirates of the Caribbean."
She watched 10 minutes.

00:01:58.334 --> 00:01:59.934
She was far too terrified.
She said, "I don't --

00:01:59.934 --> 00:02:01.367
I don't want to see you
doing that."

00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:03.167
[ Laughter ]
-Really?

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:04.467
-Yeah, she wasn't impressed.

00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:06.167
But do you know
what she also said?

00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:07.901
She said,
"No, I don't like you in that.

00:02:07.901 --> 00:02:09.234
You're too pretty."
I was like, "Oh."

00:02:09.234 --> 00:02:11.834
But she said, "But don't worry,
you're not anymore."

00:02:11.834 --> 00:02:13.434
[ Audience "Ohhs" ]
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:13.434 --> 00:02:15.133
-Wow!
Brutal.

00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:16.868
-"Mwah! Go to sleep,
go to sleep." Right?

00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:17.968
-Yeah, "I love you, I love you."

00:02:17.968 --> 00:02:19.267
-Yeah.
"You were cute for a second."

00:02:19.267 --> 00:02:20.501
-Yeah, exactly.
-Have any of them

00:02:20.501 --> 00:02:22.734
shown any interest in acting?
-No. No.

00:02:22.734 --> 00:02:24.634
I actually asked her.
I said, "You know, do --

00:02:24.634 --> 00:02:26.300
You know, is that something --"
We took her to the theater.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:27.834
I said, "Is that something
that you want to do?"

00:02:27.834 --> 00:02:30.601
She was like, "No!"
I went, "Why not?"

00:02:30.601 --> 00:02:32.501
Said, "All those people
were looking at you!"

00:02:32.501 --> 00:02:33.701
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.701 --> 00:02:35.901
And I was like,
"You know, I'm quite proud."

00:02:35.901 --> 00:02:37.467
-Yeah. Yeah, yeah, good.
Don't get involved.

00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:38.534
-"Good point."
-Yeah.

00:02:38.534 --> 00:02:40.400
-Yeah.
-You started acting at around

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.334
7 years old, right?
-I did -- 6, yes.

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:45.367
-6. In something called, I want
to say, "Royal Celebration"?

00:02:45.367 --> 00:02:47.133
-It was. It was called
"A Royal Celebration."

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:49.200
I don't think I did very much.
I think I sort of ran in

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.767
and said, "Mummy, Daddy."
You know, but I loved it.

00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:53.734
-Did you have any -- "Mommy,
Daddy," were your lines?

00:02:53.734 --> 00:02:56.200
-I mean, I don't remember.
I remember very few lines.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.667
I remember there being,
you know, there were marks

00:02:57.667 --> 00:02:59.901
on the floor where you have
to hit when you're acting on --

00:02:59.901 --> 00:03:02.133
on -- on film,
and I remember mine was green,

00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:03.834
and I was so pleased
about hitting my mark

00:03:03.834 --> 00:03:05.467
and just being there.

00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:07.634
-And it was about
Princess Diana and Charles?

00:03:07.634 --> 00:03:10.267
-It was about a street party
on the day

00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:11.701
that Charles and Diana
got married.

00:03:11.701 --> 00:03:13.100
-Wow.
-Yeah.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.667
-This was your acting debut?
-This was my acting debut, yeah.

00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:16.901
-We found the clip.
We have it.

00:03:16.901 --> 00:03:18.467
[ Cheers and applause ]
Your acting debut.

00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:20.367
-No.
-And you did have a line.

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:22.167
-Did I?
-Yeah. Here is 6-year-old

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:24.367
Keira Knightley
making her acting debut

00:03:24.367 --> 00:03:27.200
in "Royal Celebration."
Check this out.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.100
-...their new life together.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.100
-Here, here, here, here!

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.400
-Everybody's been waiting for.

00:03:33.968 --> 00:03:35.901
-Wow!
-Wow!

00:03:35.901 --> 00:03:40.100
[ Cheers and applause ]
Wow, yeah.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:43.934
I mean, I had one word,
which I repeated many times.

00:03:43.934 --> 00:03:45.133
So, I clearly -- I milked it.
Yeah.

00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:46.267
-"Here, here, here, here!"
-Yeah.

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:48.334
-And then smash cut to --
totally different.

00:03:48.334 --> 00:03:50.634
-Glum.
-You got your one line over.

00:03:50.634 --> 00:03:52.334
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
Out.

00:03:52.334 --> 00:03:56.033
-Just -- We were looking at IMDb
today, and just 20 years,

00:03:56.033 --> 00:03:57.868
almost to the day,
was the release of

00:03:57.868 --> 00:04:00.467
"Bend It Like Beckham."
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:01.868
Isn't that crazy?

00:04:01.868 --> 00:04:04.200
I feel like just yesterday
you did that film.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.968
Did you know that it was
gonna be a giant hit?

00:04:06.968 --> 00:04:08.534
Or did you feel like it was
going to be your breakout?

00:04:08.534 --> 00:04:11.033
-No. No, no, I mean, I literally
remember telling people

00:04:11.033 --> 00:04:12.367
that I was doing it
and saying, you know,

00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:13.634
"It's called
'Bend It Like Beckham,'"

00:04:13.634 --> 00:04:16.167
and them going, "Oh,
that's really embarrassing."

00:04:16.167 --> 00:04:17.367
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:18.767
-Really?
-Okay. Yeah, yeah.

00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:20.300
No, it was -- no. And they were
all like, "Don't worry.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:21.534
Nobody will see it.
It's fine."

00:04:21.534 --> 00:04:22.601
-"Don't worry.
Nobody will see it."

00:04:22.601 --> 00:04:24.100
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
No, it was --

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.434
No, but it was the idea of it,
because you know women's soccer

00:04:26.434 --> 00:04:28.133
was not as big back then.
-Yeah.

00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:31.267
-And so, the idea of the whole
thing was sort of ridiculous.

00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:33.767
And it's amazing, because it's
still the film, even today,

00:04:33.767 --> 00:04:35.934
you know, if somebody comes up
and talks to me about my work,

00:04:35.934 --> 00:04:37.200
it's that one.
It's so loved.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.434
-Yeah.
-It's like -- it's amazing.

00:04:38.434 --> 00:04:40.067
-Yeah, it is beloved.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:40.067 --> 00:04:41.100
You've done some great work,
though.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:42.934
-Thank you.
-I want to talk about

00:04:42.934 --> 00:04:45.501
your new one,
"Boston Strangler."

00:04:45.501 --> 00:04:48.434
It's out on Hulu this Friday.
It's based on the true story.

00:04:48.434 --> 00:04:51.000
How much about the story
did you know?

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.334
-I didn't really know very much.
So, I'd obviously heard of it.

00:04:53.334 --> 00:04:55.133
-Yeah.
-So -- but really,

00:04:55.133 --> 00:04:56.734
I just sort of read the script
and thought, "Whoa.

00:04:56.734 --> 00:04:58.267
This is such
a complicated story.

00:04:58.267 --> 00:05:00.367
And so interesting."
And what I didn't realize

00:05:00.367 --> 00:05:02.868
was that there are
these two female journalists

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:04.434
right at the center
of the story.

00:05:04.434 --> 00:05:05.767
And actually,
when you google it, you know,

00:05:05.767 --> 00:05:08.167
they were the two that really
kind of went after it.

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:09.868
They first linked the killings.

00:05:09.868 --> 00:05:12.234
They even named the killer
"The Boston Strangler."

00:05:12.234 --> 00:05:14.767
And yet, when you google it,
their kind of part of

00:05:14.767 --> 00:05:17.534
that history
has largely been erased.

00:05:17.534 --> 00:05:19.000
So, it felt like
a really interesting --

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.968
You know, it's a true-crime --
crime piece.

00:05:20.968 --> 00:05:23.701
It felt -- It felt cool to
kind of see it through that

00:05:23.701 --> 00:05:25.567
female gaze
and to put these two journalists

00:05:25.567 --> 00:05:26.801
sort of in the center of it.

00:05:26.801 --> 00:05:29.133
-And it's shot in Boston.
And, yeah.

00:05:29.133 --> 00:05:31.734
Did you do a Boston accent?
-No.

00:05:31.734 --> 00:05:32.968
-That's a smart move.

00:05:32.968 --> 00:05:35.234
-Well, I wanted to do
the Boston accent.

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:37.000
-[ Laughs ]
-Who talked you out?

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.033
-The director was like,
"Abso--" He's from Boston.

00:05:39.033 --> 00:05:41.133
-Oh, yeah.
No, people from Boston --

00:05:41.133 --> 00:05:42.734
-No. You can't --
I mean, if you weren't

00:05:42.734 --> 00:05:45.334
from the street that the
character was from in Boston,

00:05:45.334 --> 00:05:47.100
you were not allowed
to do the Boston accent.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.501
So every single actor
went in going, "Boston accent!

00:05:50.501 --> 00:05:51.868
It's our time!"
-You're like, "No, no, no, no!

00:05:51.868 --> 00:05:52.968
No, but everyone's
made this mistake!"

00:05:52.968 --> 00:05:54.200
-Yeah, yeah, no,
we were not allowed.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.767
So it's a standard
American accent.

00:05:55.767 --> 00:05:57.133
[ Laughter ]
There is no Boston.

00:05:57.133 --> 00:05:58.901
-That is a very smart move
that you did that.

00:05:58.901 --> 00:06:01.300
I was in a Boston movie.
I did not do a Boston accent,

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:04.167
but I did do it
when my character got excited.

00:06:04.167 --> 00:06:05.734
Like rea--
-Yeah. You've got it in there?

00:06:05.734 --> 00:06:07.133
-Oh, yeah.
-Really?

00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:09.133
-If my character was drunk
or excited, I threw a Boston.

00:06:09.133 --> 00:06:10.801
-You threw it. And nobody went,
"You're not allowed"?

00:06:10.801 --> 00:06:12.267
-No, it worked.
It was perfect.

00:06:12.267 --> 00:06:13.400
I actually was yelling it.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:14.701
Because Boston,
you can't mess it up.

00:06:14.701 --> 00:06:15.968
-No, you can't.
-And it lives forever.

00:06:15.968 --> 00:06:17.434
And they'll find you
on the streets and be like,

00:06:17.434 --> 00:06:18.934
"That accent was terrible."
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:18.934 --> 00:06:20.567
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
They're very vocal about it.

00:06:20.567 --> 00:06:22.334
-Yelling from the windows of
Dunkin' Donuts at you.

00:06:22.334 --> 00:06:23.501
Yeah, absolutely.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.501 --> 00:06:25.434
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-I want to show a clip.

00:06:25.434 --> 00:06:27.033
Here's Keira Knightley in
"Boston Strangler."

00:06:27.033 --> 00:06:28.367
Take a look.

00:06:28.367 --> 00:06:30.100
-They won't coordinate
with other police departments.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:31.334
Connections are being missed,

00:06:31.334 --> 00:06:33.000
and good leads are falling
through the cracks.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.667
-Isn't some of that
to be expected?

00:06:34.667 --> 00:06:36.467
-We are way beyond that.

00:06:36.467 --> 00:06:37.801
Oh, I'm sorry, Jack.
Am I keeping you up?

00:06:37.801 --> 00:06:38.834
-Will you cut the [bleep]
already?

00:06:38.834 --> 00:06:40.067
What's the story?

00:06:40.067 --> 00:06:41.501
-The city can't protect
its women.

00:06:41.501 --> 00:06:42.968
-Come on.
-What?

00:06:42.968 --> 00:06:44.901
You don't think it's true, or
you don't think it's a story?

00:06:44.901 --> 00:06:46.200
-What the hell is it with you?

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:47.734
You can't go after
someone's livelihood

00:06:47.734 --> 00:06:50.133
because you're impatient.
-10 women are dead,

00:06:50.133 --> 00:06:51.934
and these [bleep]
don't have a clue.

00:06:51.934 --> 00:06:53.601
-You don't have a story.

00:06:53.601 --> 00:06:54.968
You have a grudge.

00:06:54.968 --> 00:06:57.000
-How many women have to die
before it's a story?

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.834
-That's enough.

00:06:58.834 --> 00:07:01.067
These are good men
busting their asses.

00:07:01.067 --> 00:07:03.400
I'm not waging a war
on the police department.

00:07:05.367 --> 00:07:08.067
-Yeah.
Keira Knightley, everybody.

00:07:08.067 --> 00:07:11.634
"Boston Strangler" is available
on Hulu this Friday.

00:07:11.634 --> 00:07:13.300
Zachary Levi joins us
after the break.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:14.801
Stick around, everybody.

